i.e., \(\quad \frac{1}{2} \mathrm{OA} \cdot \mathrm{CD}<\frac{x}{2 \pi} \cdot \pi \cdot(\mathrm{OA})^{2}<\frac{1}{2} \mathrm{OA} \cdot \mathrm{AB}\).
i.e., \(\mathrm{CD}<x . \mathrm{OA}<\mathrm{AB}\).
From \(\triangle \mathrm{OCD}\), \(\sin x=\frac{\mathrm{CD}}{\mathrm{OA}}(\) since \(\mathrm{OC}=\mathrm{OA})\) and hence \(\mathrm{CD}=\mathrm{OA} \sin x\). Also \(\tan x=\frac{\mathrm{AB}}{\mathrm{OA}}\) and hence
\[\mathrm{AB}=\mathrm{OA} \cdot \tan x . \text { Thus }\]

Since length OA is positive, we have
\[\sin x<x<\tan x .\]

Since \(0<x<\frac{\pi}{2}, \sin x\) is positive and thus by dividing throughout by \(\sin x\), we have
\[\begin{aligned}
& 1<\frac{x}{\sin x}<\frac{1}{\cos x} \text {. Taking reciprocals throughout, we have } \\
